      "writtenOn": "2022-09-28T19:57:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s not ideal, no, but it\u0027s historically how we have always done it (skslc and SkSLPrecompile have both always passed kFragment when compiling a module). In practice, a symbol like `sk_ThreadPosition` is defined in the compute module itself; it\u0027s not a true intrinsic, so that one would be fine. The problem comes about with statements like `discard`, or features like unsized-array, which really are gated on program kind. That is where you\u0027d see an issue.\n\nIn practice, as I discovered earlier, if you violate the program-kind rules in a module, it will be caught and reported at golden-test generation time even if it\u0027s not caught here. \n\nI can cut a bug to investigate this further but I\u0027m not sure there\u0027s much value-add compared to finishing the minifier.",
